    "done_when": "The fragment docs/evidence/fragments/a-project-reads-as-one-picture/remeasure-the-picture-reads-on-three-mounts.html records 12 calls: the four questions (what is critical, what blocks a named section, who holds a named file, what should run next) on each of power_convertor, nova and reckon. Each call is made through the served read path, the reckon.mcp tool function under the request scopes _deadline_tool(request_scope=True) enters, never an inner function called directly. Each runs on the fleet node with a writable RECKON_HOME under node-local /tmp, warmed by one discarded call per mount first, and each record carries its command, elapsed seconds and answer. All 12 complete within the 30 s read deadline, or are listed as failures with their cause, taken from an interrupt traceback under timeout -s INT. Every disagreement with the owning coordinator's account is counted and classified as a wiring fault or a derivation defect. The first measure's evidence is at /reckon/evidence/archive/a-project-reads-as-one-picture-landed.html#s8-measure and its correction at #s8-measure-correction. The base-commit control on the same power_convertor snapshot disagrees at least on the critical path and the first-ranked item. The removed-edge control removes one declared cross-plan edge that lies on the critical path of whichever of the three mounts has one (name the mount and the edge). The critical path must move, and an undeclared-wait finding is raised where a blocker still names the removed edge. If none of the three mounts has a cross-plan edge on its critical path, say so with the critical paths you read, and the control is recorded as not applicable. Copies of snapshots go to node-local /tmp and are deleted afterwards, printing what was deleted. Run everything in the foreground on the fleet allocation.",
